  {
   "id": 2,
   "ref": "74–79",
   "en": "One moment Dido leads Aeneas through the heart of the city, showing him her Phoenician wealth and the city already built; she begins to speak, and stops halfway through the words. The next, as the day fades, she calls for the same feast all over again, and in her madness begs to hear once more the sufferings of Troy, and hangs once more on the speaker's every word.",
   "en_marked": "[One moment|1] {Dido|Added for clarity: the Latin only has the verb ending, so the translator names Dido.} [leads|7] [Aeneas|3] [through|5] the [heart|2] of the [city|6], [showing him|9] her [Phoenician|8] [wealth|10] [and|11] the [city|11] [already built|12]; [she begins|13] [to speak|14], [and|15] [stops|18] [halfway|15] [through|16] the [words|17]. [The next|19], as the [day|22] [fades|21], [she calls for|24] the [same|20] [feast|23] {all over again|Not in the Latin. Added to stress the repetition already implied by eadem, 'the same'.}, [and|25] [in her madness|27] [begs|30] [to hear|28] [once more|26] the [sufferings|29] [of Troy|25], [and hangs|31] [once more|32] [on|34] the [speaker's|33] {every|Not in the Latin. Added to bring out how intently Dido listens to Aeneas.} [word|35].",
