  {
   "id": 3,
   "ref": "80–85",
   "en": "Later, once they have parted, and the fading moon in turn dims her light and the setting stars urge sleep, she lies grieving, alone in the empty house, stretched out on the couch he has left behind. Lost within herself, she hears him and sees him, absent though he is; or takes Ascanius onto her lap, caught by his likeness to his father, and keeps him there, hoping she might somehow deceive a love she cannot speak of.",
   "en_marked": "[Later|1], [once|2] [they have parted|3], [and|4] the [fading|5] [moon|7] [in turn|6] [dims|8] her [light|4] [and|9] the [setting|10] [stars|11] [urge|9] [sleep|12], [she lies|19] [grieving|15], [alone|13] in the [empty|16] [house|14], [stretched out on|19] the [couch|17] {he|Added for clarity: relictis only means 'left behind', so the translator supplies Aeneas as the one who left.} [has left behind|18]. [Lost within herself|21], [she hears|23] [him|20] [and|24] [sees|24] [him|20], [absent though he is|22]; [or|25] [takes|31] [Ascanius|27] onto her [lap|26], [caught|30] by his [likeness|29] to his [father|28], and [keeps him there|31], [hoping|33] [she might|35] {somehow|Not in the Latin. Added to bring out the desperate hope in si possit.} [deceive|34] a [love|36] [she cannot speak of|32].",
