 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "np6-01",
   "cat": "order",
   "label": "Emphatic position",
   "quote": "uritur",
   "trans": "she burns",
   "line": "68",
   "body": "The verb opens the section in a heavy, single word: Dido's burning comes before her name, so passion defines her before anything else.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np6-02",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Epithet",
   "quote": "infelix Dido",
   "trans": "unhappy Dido",
   "line": "68",
   "body": "infelix, \"ill-starred\", is Virgil's recurring epithet for Dido. It marks her out as doomed and invites the reader's pity from the start.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np6-03",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Enjambment",
   "quote": "totaque vagatur urbe furens",
   "trans": "wanders through the whole city, raving",
   "line": "68–69",
   "body": "urbe furens runs over into the next line, so her wandering spills across the line break just as it spills through the city.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np6-04",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Madness",
   "quote": "furens",
   "trans": "raving",
   "line": "69",
   "body": "furens is the language of madness (furor): Dido is not simply in love but out of control, a theme that grows through the book.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np6-05",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Simile",
   "quote": "qualis coniecta cerva sagitta",
   "trans": "like a deer struck by an arrow",
   "line": "69",
   "body": "Dido is compared to a wounded deer, an animal that is hunted and helpless. The image turns her love into an injury that will prove fatal.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np6-06",
   "cat": "order",
   "label": "Word order",
   "quote": "coniecta cerva sagitta",
   "trans": "a deer struck by an arrow",
   "line": "69",
   "body": "The arrow (sagitta) and its participle coniecta frame the deer (cerva), so the word order pins her in place just as the arrow does.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np6-07",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Dramatic irony",
   "quote": "pastor agens telis liquitque volatile ferrum nescius",
   "trans": "a shepherd, hunting with his weapons, left the flying iron in her, not knowing",
   "line": "71–72",
   "body": "The shepherd who wounds without knowing it stands for Aeneas, who does not yet realise what he has done to Dido. nescius is held back to the start of a line for emphasis.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np6-08",
   "cat": "sound",
   "label": "Alliteration",
   "quote": "fuga silvas saltusque",
   "trans": "in flight through the woods and glades",
   "line": "72",
   "body": "The repeated s sounds give a rushing, breathless effect to the deer's flight.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np6-09",
   "cat": "order",
   "label": "Emphatic position",
   "quote": "haeret lateri letalis harundo",
   "trans": "the deadly arrow sticks in her side",
   "line": "73",
   "body": "The simile ends on the arrow still fixed in her, and letalis (\"deadly\") looks ahead to Dido's death. The l sounds slow the line to a close.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np6-10",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Anaphora",
   "quote": "nunc … nunc",
   "trans": "now … now",
   "line": "74–77",
   "body": "The repeated nunc divides Dido's days into restless phases, showing how she swings from one activity to another.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np6-11",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Characterisation",
   "quote": "Sidoniasque ostentat opes urbemque paratam",
   "trans": "shows off her Phoenician wealth and the city already built",
   "line": "75",
   "body": "ostentat means \"shows off\": Dido displays her city as a way of winning Aeneas, perhaps hoping that he will settle there rather than found his own.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np6-12",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Vivid detail",
   "quote": "incipit effari mediaque in voce resistit",
   "trans": "she begins to speak and stops in mid-sentence",
   "line": "76",
   "body": "Dido cannot finish a sentence. The broken-off speech shows her confusion and shyness in front of Aeneas.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np6-13",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Repetition",
   "quote": "iterum … iterum",
   "trans": "again … again",
   "line": "78–79",
   "body": "The doubled iterum shows Dido asking for the same story over and over, hungry for anything that keeps Aeneas near her.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np6-14",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Madness",
   "quote": "demens",
   "trans": "out of her mind",
   "line": "78",
   "body": "demens repeats the idea of furens: her obsession with Aeneas' story is presented as a kind of madness.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np6-15",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Vivid detail",
   "quote": "pendetque iterum narrantis ab ore",
   "trans": "hangs once more on the lips of the speaker",
   "line": "79",
   "body": "Dido literally \"hangs\" from his mouth, a vivid picture of how completely she is absorbed in him.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np6-16",
   "cat": "sound",
   "label": "Atmosphere",
   "quote": "suadentque cadentia sidera somnos",
   "trans": "and the setting stars urge sleep",
   "line": "81",
   "body": "Virgil reuses a line from Aeneas' story in Book 2. The soft s sounds and slow rhythm create a quiet night, which contrasts with Dido's sleepless grief.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np6-17",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Pathos",
   "quote": "sola domo maeret vacua",
   "trans": "alone she grieves in the empty house",
   "line": "82",
   "body": "sola and vacua stress her loneliness once Aeneas has left the room, even though he is only elsewhere in the palace.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np6-18",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Polyptoton",
   "quote": "illum absens absentem auditque videtque",
   "trans": "apart, she hears and sees him apart from her",
   "line": "83",
   "body": "absens absentem places the two absent lovers side by side, and the double -que gives the sense of a vision she cannot shake off.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np6-19",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Pathos",
   "quote": "gremio Ascanium genitoris imagine capta detinet",
   "trans": "she holds Ascanius on her lap, captivated by his likeness to his father",
   "line": "84–85",
   "body": "Dido clings to the child because he looks like Aeneas. Readers of Book 1 know that Cupid took Ascanius' form there, so the scene is doubly charged.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
