 "section": 6,
 "questions": [
  "The simile of the wounded deer (69–73) is full of details: the shepherd who does not know he has hit her, the arrow that stays in her side. How far does each detail match Dido's situation, and who is the \"shepherd\"?",
  "Lines 74–79 move between \"nunc … nunc\". What does this pattern suggest about Dido's state of mind, and about how she is spending her days?",
  "How does Virgil show that Dido's love is already damaging her role as queen, even before the next section tells us so directly?"
 ],
 "context": [
  {
   "h": "The wounded deer",
   "body": "<p>Virgil sets the simile in Crete, famous for its archers and hunters, and in the woods of Mount Dicte. The details are chosen with care. The shepherd shoots from a distance and never learns that he has hit anything: Aeneas has done Dido harm without meaning to and without knowing it. The deer runs, but cannot escape the arrow fixed in her side, just as Dido's restless wandering through the city carries her wound with her.</p><p>The simile also looks back to Book 1, where Venus appeared to Aeneas dressed as a huntress, and forward to the hunt of lines 117–128, where Dido rides out to the hunt that will lead to the cave.</p>"
  }
 ]
}